CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure plots the probability simplex for a three-class problem on a set of three-dimensional coordinate axes. The axes are black arrows radiating from a shared origin: y_1 runs to the right, y_2 runs straight up, and y_3 runs down and to the left toward the viewer. Each axis carries a single tick labeled 1, and each unit point is annotated with a bold class name, Car at y_1 equal to 1, Bike at y_2 equal to 1, and Bird at y_3 equal to 1. A translucent pink triangle is filled in with its three corners resting exactly on those unit points, and the stretches of axis inside the triangle are drawn darker where the shading passes over them. The slanted face, not the volume it cuts off, is what carries the meaning: only points on that plane are admissible, the geometric form of requiring three nonnegative scores that sum to one. The drawing accompanies the definition of a classifier as a mapping from RGB images into the (K-1)-dimensional simplex, here with K equal to 3, where each vertex is an unambiguous prediction of one class and interior points are graded mixtures.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Origin at (0,0,0) where the three axes meet
  - Black axis arrow y1 running to screen-right, past the unit point, with a solid arrowhead
  - Black axis arrow y2 running straight up, past the unit point, with a solid arrowhead
  - Black axis arrow y3 running down-and-left toward the viewer, past the unit point, with a solid arrowhead
  - Italic axis labels 'y1' (right of the y1 arrowhead), 'y2' (above the y2 arrowhead), 'y3' (below-left of the y3 arrowhead)
  - Tick label '1' at each unit point: left of (1,0,0), left of (0,1,0), above-left of (0,0,1)
  - Bold class labels: 'Car' below-right of the y1 unit point, 'Bike' right of the y2 unit point, 'Bird' below-right of the y3 unit point
  - Translucent pink filled triangle with corners exactly at (1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1)
  - Dark red segments of each axis from the origin out to its unit point (the portions the triangle shades over)
  - Draggable probability point marker constrained to the triangle interior
  - Three thin guide lines / dropped coordinates from the draggable point to the three axes (shown while dragging)
  - Numeric readout panel showing y1, y2, y3 and their sum = 1

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: 1 probability unit = 1 world unit. Map y1 -> +X (screen-right), y2 -> +Y (screen-up), y3 -> +Z (receding diagonal that projects down-and-left toward the viewer under the standard isometric pose; if the default pose sends +Z up-right, negate it so the Bird axis reads down-left as in the source). Axis shafts run from the origin out to 1.45 units with cone arrowheads ~0.09 long, 0.045 radius, line width ~4px; scene spans roughly -0.2..1.6 on each axis. Triangle vertices exactly (1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1), rendered double-sided, flat-shaded, no lighting. Colors: axes beyond the unit points pure black #000000; the origin-to-unit-point stretch of each axis dark maroon #5A0F0F (it reads darker because the pink fill passes over it); triangle fill #F7B3B3 at ~0.75 opacity (source is a light salmon pink, no stroke on the triangle edges other than where they coincide with the axes); draggable marker a solid dark red #B11226 sphere of radius ~0.045; guide lines thin dashed #B11226 at 0.5 opacity. Labels are black, rendered as screen-facing sprites: italic serif 'y1' right of the +X arrowhead, 'y2' just above the +Y arrowhead, 'y3' below-left of the y3 arrowhead; serif '1' tick numerals placed just inside/left of each unit point; bold sans class names 'Car' below-right of (1,0,0), 'Bike' immediately right of (0,1,0), 'Bird' below-right of (0,0,1). Point constraint math: barycentric coords (y1,y2,y3) with yi >= 0 and y1+y2+y3 = 1; project any dragged 3D position onto the plane x+y+z=1 then clamp into the triangle. Default point position is the centroid (1/3, 1/3, 1/3).

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The pink triangle is a flat slanted plane slicing the positive octant, not a solid body — rotating until it is seen edge-on shows that all admissible score vectors lie on a single 2D surface in 3D space."
  },
  {
    "id": "dragProbabilityPoint",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "Moving the point anywhere on the triangle changes the three class confidences continuously, but y1 + y2 + y3 always stays exactly 1 — the simplex is the geometric form of the nonnegativity-plus-sum-to-one constraint, so confidence can only be traded between classes, never created."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectVertex",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Clicking Car, Bike, or Bird snaps the point to that corner, driving the other two coordinates to 0 — a vertex is a one-hot, unambiguous prediction, while everything strictly inside is a graded mixture of classes."
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "Viewing the same point in the ambient 3D axes versus flat-on the triangle shows that a 3-class distribution really has only K-1 = 2 degrees of freedom.",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "3D axes",
      "Flat-on simplex"
    ],
    "default": "3D axes"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The constraint plane",
    "narration": "Three axes y1, y2, y3 carry the scores for Car, Bike and Bird. The pink triangle joins the three unit points: every point on it has nonnegative coordinates summing to 1.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D axes"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mixtures inside",
    "narration": "Drag the marker around the interior. The readouts shift — more Car means less Bike and Bird — but the sum is pinned at 1, so confidence is only ever redistributed.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D axes"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Vertices are certain answers",
    "narration": "Click 'Car'. The point snaps to (1,0,0) and the other two coordinates collapse to zero: a corner of the simplex is an unambiguous single-class prediction.",
    "state": {
